Spec review, round 2, track closeout_nested_subboxes_20261124, HEAD 9c8d63a. Review status: pass (0 Blocker, 1 Major, 5 Nit). F1 to F11 of round 1 are resolved. See the JSON for the evidence.

### What this track proposes
The track changes `plan_closeout_feedback` so an indented `[ ]` or `[~]` box gives no `plan task is not complete` line. The box is ignored when its nearest shallower parent is an `[x]` line, covered or not, or an ignored nested open box. Top-level open tasks, boxes under non-ignored open parents, and boxes under `deferred` parents keep today's feedback. An ignored box is stored with covered False and ignored True, and a `[x]` box under it takes coverage from its own SHA or the nearest covered `[x]` ancestor. A new Red test, a rewrite of case 3 of `test_subbox_sha.py`, and README and CHANGELOG edits go in; `track_incomplete`, `phase_counts`, and the jr-green branch stay unchanged.

### How the current system works, per this spec
`plan_closeout_feedback` reads each checkbox line and keeps `(indent, status, covered)` for each line. It appends `plan task is not complete` for each non-`[x]` line at any depth, before the parent-cover rule runs, except for `deferred` lines. A nested `[x]` line is covered by its own SHA or by a covered `[x]` line that is its nearest parent. `track_incomplete` and `phase_counts` read only column-0 lines, so they already ignore nested boxes. Only `closeout_candidates` and `closeout_feedback` call `plan_closeout_feedback`, and the Math plan fails closeout today with 302 feedback lines.

### Findings
- F12 [Major] spec.md FR-3 and FR-2: FR-3 fixes each `processed` entry at four values `(indent, status, covered, ignored)`, with `ignored=False` when a line is not ignored. A `deferred` line then has the same four values as an ordinary `[x]` line. The checker stores it as `(indent, status, True)`, or `(indent, status, False)` for a closeout line without a SHA. FR-1(a) ignores a box under any `[x]` line, and FR-2 forbids it under a `deferred` parent. Probe: `- [x] T (deferred)` plus `  - [ ] s` gives one `plan task is not complete` line today and none by the four values. Rationale: a Green that follows FR-3 fails the deferred `[x]` fixture of Task 1.1, so the implementer must add a fifth fact or recode `status`. A recode of `status` changes today's result for `- [x] T (deferred)` plus `  - [x] s` (empty feedback). The revision removed the round 1 sentences that kept `deferred` handling and the SHA rule of nested `[x]` boxes, and no fixture checks them. State the behavior and drop the tuple layout, or add a `deferred` value, and keep `deferred` handling and `[x]` coverage outside an ignored chain as today.
- F13 [Nit] spec.md FR-1: The sentence 'A SHA-less `[x]` parent still reports its own `completed plan task lacks commit SHA` line; its nested boxes add no second line' says 'boxes', not 'open boxes'. A nested `[x]` box under a SHA-less `[x]` parent still gets its own SHA line. Probe: `- [x] T`, `  - [x] a`, `  - [x] b`, `    - [ ] c`, `  - [ ] d` gives three SHA lines (T, a, b) and no other line. Rationale: the wording conflicts with FR-3 and with case 2 of `test_subbox_sha.py`, which stays and expects two lines. Write 'nested open boxes'.
- F14 [Nit] spec.md Acceptance Criteria and plan.md Task 1.1: The case 'a `[x]` box without a SHA under an ignored `[ ]` box ... the SHA message with an uncovered ancestor' does not give the exact list. The rule gives two lines: the `completed plan task lacks commit SHA` line of the uncovered `[x]` ancestor and the same message for the box. Probe: `- [x] T`, `  - [ ] s`, `    - [x] ss` gives [T, ss]. Rationale: Task 1.1 says each case names an exact list, and a Red that expects one line fails after Green. State the two-line list.
- F15 [Nit] plan.md Task 1.1: The `closeout_candidates` case names no fixture tracks and no expected list. Class 2 of `test_subbox_sha.py` builds `expected` from `plan_closeout_feedback`, which is true by construction because `closeout_candidates` calls it. Only the fixed list `["passes"]` checks the rule. Rationale: without a fixed list and a track with a SHA'd `[x]` parent and a nested `[ ]` box, the case passes before Green. Name the tracks and the expected list.
- F16 [Nit] spec.md FR-5 and plan.md Task 1.2: The plan updates 'the README.md Status sentence', but README.md holds two `As of` Status sentences: line 33 (2026-11-18, Sub-box SHA rule) and line 34 (2026-11-19, Measure board). Neither says that unticked boxes fail, and line 33 stays true after the change. The plan names no new text. Rationale: the implementer must pick the sentence and the text. The archive commits of earlier tracks rewrite this sentence (bf96031, 4b3d82e), so closeout changes it again. Name the sentence and its text, or move the README edit to closeout.
- F17 [Nit] spec.md FR-1 and FR-2: A `[x]` line nested under an open top-level task also grants the ignore by FR-1(a). FR-2 says that a box under a non-ignored open parent fails, and that 'this holds under a top-level `[ ]` or `[~]`'. Probe: `- [ ] T`, `  - [x] a <sha>`, `    - [ ] c` gives only the line of T, so `c` is ignored. Rationale: no fixture checks this, and a reader of the FR-2 sentence can expect `c` to fail. The verdict does not change, because T still fails. Add one fixture or one sentence.

Notes for the orchestrator:
- Round 1 findings F1 to F11 are resolved. No finding carries a CONTEST item, so nothing is withdrawn or kept for that reason.
- F12 is Major, not Blocker. FR-2 and the named deferred fixtures pin every observable result. The gap is an internal data layout, and the named fixture fails a wrong Green. The five Nits are open Nits and accepted as Nits.
- Every named fixture of Task 1.1 matches the result of the specified rule when the fixture has no `## Phase` heading. Math lines 38-50 give []; Math line 52 plus lines 53-60 give one SHA line. The deferred fixtures add `deferred closeout task lacks done SHA` when the plan has a `## Phase` heading, so Red must write them without one.
- The Math plan after the rule gives 102 lines (71 lacks SHA, 31 not complete) against 302 today, so it still fails closeout for the reasons in the Overview.
- The only test that asserts the old rule is `test_subbox_sha.py` case 3, and the plan edits it. Other tests, plugin tests, mod fixtures, and docs hold no assertion of the old rule. CHANGELOG.md line 47 holds the old sentence.
- The measure-guard denied a probe with less-than and greater-than characters in the command, so all probes used the operator module and wrote no file.

